<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>Graphs Representing Exponential Decay</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01465</md:content-id>
    <md:title>Graphs Representing Exponential Decay</md:title>
    <md:uuid>d71f5909-46f8-411d-866b-a01e4393d45e</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title>Activity</title>
      <note class="link-to-learning" id="note-00001">
        <para id="para-00001">Log into <link url="https://student.desmos.com">student.desmos.com</link>
        using the information provided by your teacher to complete the activity.</para>
      </note>

      <para id="para-00024" class="custom-question-container"><span class="custom-question-number">1. </span>Examine the graph of the functions below.</para>
      <para id="para-00007">
            <media alt=".">
              <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_14_06_01a.svg"/>
            </media>
          </para>
      <para id="para-00027">Match each of the functions with a graph. Be prepared to explain your reasoning. </para>
      <exercise id="exer-00005">
        <problem id="prob-00006">
          <para id="para-00028">a. Which graph best represents
            <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>m</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mn>200</m:mn>
                <m:mo>⋅</m:mo>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mfrac>
                  <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                  <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                </m:mfrac>
                <m:msup>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                </m:msup>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>?
          </para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00005">
          <para id="para-00029">Graph D</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <exercise id="exer-00006">
        <problem id="prob-00007">
          <para id="para-00030">b. Which graph best represents
            <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>n</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mn>200</m:mn>
                <m:mo>⋅</m:mo>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mfrac>
                  <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                </m:mfrac>
                <m:msup>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                </m:msup>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>?
          </para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00006">
          <para id="para-00031">Graph C</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <exercise id="exer-00007">
        <problem id="prob-00008">
          <para id="para-00032">c. Which graph best represents
            <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>p</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mn>200</m:mn>
                <m:mo>⋅</m:mo>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mfrac>
                  <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                  <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                </m:mfrac>
                <m:msup>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                </m:msup>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>?
          </para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00007">
          <para id="para-00033">Graph B</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <exercise id="exer-00008">
        <problem id="prob-00009">
          <para id="para-00034">d. Which graph best represents
            <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>q</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mn>200</m:mn>
                <m:mo>⋅</m:mo>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mfrac>
                  <m:mn>7</m:mn>
                  <m:mn>8</m:mn>
                </m:mfrac>
                <m:msup>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                </m:msup>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>?
          </para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00008">
          <para id="para-00035">Graph A</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      
      
      
      <para id="para-00025" class="custom-question-container"><span class="custom-question-number">2. </span>Functions <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>f</m:mi>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> and <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>g</m:mi>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math><emphasis> </emphasis>are
          defined by these equations:</para>
      <list id="list-00003">
        <item><m:math>
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>f</m:mi>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
              <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mn>1</m:mn>
              <m:mo>,</m:mo>
              <m:mn>000</m:mn>
              <m:mi>·</m:mi>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
              <m:mfrac>
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                </m:mrow>
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mn>10</m:mn>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:mfrac>
              <m:msup>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              </m:msup>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math></item>
        <item><m:math>
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>g</m:mi>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
              <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mn>1</m:mn>
              <m:mo>,</m:mo>
              <m:mn>000</m:mn>
              <m:mi>·</m:mi>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
              <m:mfrac>
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mn>9</m:mn>
                </m:mrow>
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mn>10</m:mn>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:mfrac>
              <m:msup>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              </m:msup>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math></item>
      </list>
      <para id="para-00026">a. Use the graphing tool or technology outside the course. On the same coordinate plane,
          sketch a graph that represents the functions, <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>f</m:mi>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> and <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>g</m:mi>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>. It may be helpful to use a
          different color for each function.</para>
      <media alt="Interactive Desmos tool">
        <iframe src="../../media/desmos/56a7de02b0c3c9e3efa76644bf1d8715b0ceb097.html" width="500" height="400"/>
      </media>
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00002"/>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00003">Compare your answers:</para>
          <para id="para-00004">
            <media alt="Line graph with two curves. Curve f (orange) decreases steeply and approaches zero quickly. Curve g (teal) decreases more gradually. Both curves have labeled axes: x (horizontal) and y (vertical).">
              <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_14_06_01.jpg"/>
            </media>

          </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <!-- BEGIN ACTIVITY DROP DOWN 1 TRY -->

      <exercise id="_2d4da33c-831a-4cfe-8c72-6986754a18f6" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00005">b. Which function is decaying more quickly?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00006"><m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math></para>
        
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <!--END
      ACTIVITY DROP DOWN 1 TRY -->

      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00008">c. Explain your reasoning regarding which function decays more quickly.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00009">Compare your answers:</para>
          <para id="para-00010"><m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                <m:mo>,</m:mo>
                <m:mn>000</m:mn>
                <m:mi>·</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mfrac>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>10</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:mfrac>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                </m:msup>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>
            has a smaller base than <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>g</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                <m:mo>,</m:mo>
                <m:mn>000</m:mn>
                <m:mi>·</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mfrac>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>9</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>10</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:mfrac>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                </m:msup>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>.
            Both bases are less than 1, so both functions decrease, but <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                <m:mo>,</m:mo>
                <m:mn>000</m:mn>
                <m:mi>·</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mfrac>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mn>10</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:mfrac>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                </m:msup>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>
            decreases more rapidly. </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <note id="note-00002"><title>Building Character: Grit</title>
              <media alt="A person in business attire climbs a mountain with a briefcase and walking stick. Trophy and bullseye icons appear in the background, symbolizing achievement and reaching goals. Orange flags and clouds complete the scene.">
          <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_14_06_02.png" width="200"/>
        </media>
          
          
            <para id="para-00011">Grit predicts accomplishing challenging goals of personal significance.
        For example, grittier students are more likely to graduate from high school, and grittier
        cadets are more likely to complete their training at West Point.</para>
            <para id="para-00012">Think
        about your current sense of grit. Are the following statements true for you?</para>
            <list id="list-00005">
          <item> There is at least one subject or activity that I never get bored of thinking about. </item>
          <item> I finish whatever I begin. </item>
        </list>
            <para id="para-00013">Don’t worry
        if none of these statements are true for you. Developing this trait takes time. Your first
        step starts today!</para>


      </note>

    </section>
    <note class="self-check" id="note-00003">
      <exercise id="exer-00004">
        <problem id="prob-00005">
          <para id="para-00014"><link class="os-embed" url="#exercise/Alg1_5_12_3_SC"/></para>
        </problem>
      </exercise>
    </note>
    <section id="sect-00003">
      <title>Additional Resources</title>

      <section id="sect-00004">
        <title>Matching Exponential Functions and Graphs</title>
        <para id="para-00015">For each of the following, match each function with one of the graphs
          in the picture below.</para>
        <para id="para-00016">

          <media alt="Six labeled curves (A–F) cross at the origin on an x-y axis. Curves A and B open left, C and D open up, E and F open right. Each curve is a different color and labeled near its end.">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_14_08_01.jpg" width="300"/>
          </media>

        </para>
        <para id="para-00017">Remember, the equation for an exponential function is <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>y</m:mi>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mi>a</m:mi>
              <m:mi>·</m:mi>
              <m:msup>
                <m:mi>b</m:mi>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              </m:msup>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>. The <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>a</m:mi>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> will affect the <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>y</m:mi>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>-intercept. When <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>b</m:mi>
              <m:mo>&lt;</m:mo>
              <m:mn>1</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>, there is exponential decay.
          The closer <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>b</m:mi>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> is to 0, the faster it decays.
          The closer <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>b</m:mi>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> is to 1, the slower it decays.
          When <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>b</m:mi>
              <m:mo>&gt;</m:mo>
              <m:mn>1</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>, the larger the value of <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>b</m:mi>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>, the faster it grows.</para>
        <table class="unnumbered os-raise-wideadjustedtable" id="table-00001">
          <tgroup cols="4">
<thead><row>
                <entry>
                  <emphasis>Equation</emphasis>
                </entry>
                <entry>
                  <emphasis><m:math display="inline">
                      <m:mrow>
                        <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                      </m:mrow>
                    </m:math>
                  -intercept</emphasis>
                </entry>
                <entry>
                  <emphasis>Growth/Decay</emphasis>
                </entry>
                <entry>
                  <emphasis>Match</emphasis>
                </entry>
              </row>
              </thead><tbody>
              <row>
                <entry>
                  1. <m:math>
                        <m:mrow>
                          <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                          <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                          <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                          <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                          <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                          <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                          <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                          <m:mn>0.68</m:mn>
                          <m:msup>
                            <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                            <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                          </m:msup>
                        </m:mrow>
                      </m:math>
                </entry>
                <entry>
                  <m:math display="inline">
                    <m:mrow>
                      <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                      <m:mn>0</m:mn>
                      <m:mo>,</m:mo>
                      <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                      <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                    </m:mrow>
                  </m:math>
                </entry>
                <entry> Decay </entry>
                <entry> B </entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry>
                  2. <m:math>
                        <m:mrow>
                          <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                          <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                          <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                          <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                          <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                          <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                          <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                          <m:mn>1.28</m:mn>
                          <m:msup>
                            <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                            <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                          </m:msup>
                        </m:mrow>
                      </m:math>
                </entry>
                <entry>
                  <m:math display="inline">
                    <m:mrow>
                      <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                      <m:mn>0</m:mn>
                      <m:mo>,</m:mo>
                      <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                      <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                    </m:mrow>
                  </m:math>
                </entry>
                <entry> Growth </entry>
                <entry> F </entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry>
                  3. <m:math>
                        <m:mrow>
                          <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                          <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                          <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                          <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                          <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                          <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                          <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                          <m:mn>0.81</m:mn>
                          <m:msup>
                            <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                            <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                          </m:msup>
                        </m:mrow>
                      </m:math>
                </entry>
                <entry>
                  <m:math display="inline">
                    <m:mrow>
                      <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                      <m:mn>0</m:mn>
                      <m:mo>,</m:mo>
                      <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                      <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                    </m:mrow>
                  </m:math>
                </entry>
                <entry> Decay </entry>
                <entry> A </entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry>
                  4. <m:math>
                        <m:mrow>
                          <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                          <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                          <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                          <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                          <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                          <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                          <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                          <m:mn>1.28</m:mn>
                          <m:msup>
                            <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                            <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                          </m:msup>
                        </m:mrow>
                      </m:math>
                </entry>
                <entry>
                  <m:math display="inline">
                    <m:mrow>
                      <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                      <m:mn>0</m:mn>
                      <m:mo>,</m:mo>
                      <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                      <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                    </m:mrow>
                  </m:math>
                </entry>
                <entry> Growth </entry>
                <entry> D </entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry>
                  5. <m:math>
                        <m:mrow>
                          <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                          <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                          <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                          <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                          <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                          <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                          <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                          <m:mn>1.59</m:mn>
                          <m:msup>
                            <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                            <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                          </m:msup>
                        </m:mrow>
                      </m:math>
                </entry>
                <entry>
                  <m:math display="inline">
                    <m:mrow>
                      <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                      <m:mn>0</m:mn>
                      <m:mo>,</m:mo>
                      <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                      <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                    </m:mrow>
                  </m:math>
                </entry>
                <entry> Growth </entry>
                <entry> E </entry>
              </row>
              <row>
                <entry>
                  6. <m:math>
                        <m:mrow>
                          <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                          <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                          <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                          <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                          <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                          <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                          <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                          <m:mn>0.68</m:mn>
                          <m:msup>
                            <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                            <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                          </m:msup>
                        </m:mrow>
                      </m:math>
                </entry>
                <entry>
                  <m:math display="inline">
                    <m:mrow>
                      <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                      <m:mn>0</m:mn>
                      <m:mo>,</m:mo>
                      <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                      <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                    </m:mrow>
                  </m:math>
                </entry>
                <entry> Decay </entry>
                <entry> C </entry>
              </row>
            </tbody>
          </tgroup>
        </table>
        <note class="try" id="note-00004">
          <title> Try It: Matching Exponential Functions and Graphs</title>
        <exercise id="exer-00003">
            <problem id="prob-00004">
              <para id="para-00018">

                <media alt="Six labeled curves (A–F) cross at the origin on an x-y axis. Curves A and B open left, C and D open up, E and F open right. Each curve is a different color and labeled near its end.">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_14_08_02.jpg" width="300"/>
                </media>

              </para>
              <para id="para-00019">In the graph above, which function, <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>a</m:mi>
                    <m:mi>·</m:mi>
                    <m:msup>
                      <m:mi>b</m:mi>
                      <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    </m:msup>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>,
          has the largest value for <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>a</m:mi>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>?</para>
              <para id="para-00020"/>
            </problem>
            <solution id="sol-00004">
              <para id="para-00021">Compare your answer: </para>
              <para id="para-00022">Here is how to identify which has the largest value for <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>a</m:mi>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>:</para>
              <para id="para-00023">In the function <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>a</m:mi>
                    <m:mi>·</m:mi>
                    <m:msup>
                      <m:mi>b</m:mi>
                      <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    </m:msup>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>
          , <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>a</m:mi>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math> changes the <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>-intercept.
          The function with the largest <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>-intercept is
          C. It crosses the <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>-axis at the
          highest point.</para>
            </solution>
          </exercise>
        </note>
      </section>
    </section>

  </content>
</document>